For $a \ge 0$ and $f \neq 0$ such that $\mathfrak{b} := \mathfrak{b}(a, f)$ is a power of two the following are equivalent:
\begin{equation} 
	\exists (z_1,\ldots,z_\nu)\in [0, \mathfrak{b})^\nu \colon 
    P(a, z_1, \ldots, z_\nu) = 0 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
	\exists g \in [\mathfrak{b}, \gamma \mathfrak{b}^\alpha) \colon  \mathscr Y(a, f) \, |\, \binom{2 \mathscr X(a, f, g)}{\mathscr X(a, f, g)} \;.
\end{equation}
Moreover, (7) $\implies $ (6) already holds when $g \in [0, 2\gamma \mathfrak{b}^\alpha)$.

Given integers $b\ge 0$, $X\geq 3b$, $Y\geq \max \{ b,2^8 \}$ and $g\ge 1$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  $b$ is a power of 2 and $Y | \binom{2 X}{X}$;
\item  $\exists h,k,l,w,x,y\geq b$ such that the Diophantine conditions (11a), (11b), (11c), (11d) hold.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, the implication (proofitem:Binom) $\implies$ (proofitem:Dioph2) holds already if $X\ge b$ (rather than $X\ge 3b$).

For every $q>0$ there is a polynomial $M_q$ with the property that for all integers $A_1,\dots,A_q,R,S,T$ such that $S\neq 0$, the following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $S|T$, $R>0$ and $A_1,\dots,A_q$ are squares,
        \item $\exists n \ge 0 : \, M_q(A_1,\dots,A_q,S,T,R,n)=0$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Specifically, $M_q$ can be given as	
    \begin{equation}  \begin{split}
        &M_q(A_1,\dots,A_q,S,T,R,n)\\
        &=\prod_{\varepsilon_1,\dots,\varepsilon_q\in\{\pm1\}^q}\left(S^2n + T^2 - S^2(2R-1)\left(T^2 + X_q^q +\sum_{j=1}^q \varepsilon_j \sqrt{A_j}X_q^{j-1}\right) \right) \\
    \end{split} \end{equation}
    where $X_q:=1+\sum_{j=1}^{q}A_j^2$.
